1
00:00:00,533 --> 00:00:02,233
You just came across a useful tip

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:03,833
Your finger tapped save

3
00:00:04,066 --> 00:00:07,666
But what you saved was not knowledge but a debt your future self must deal with

4
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:08,900
The title is spot on

5
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:09,966
The tone is ruthless

6
00:00:10,066 --> 00:00:11,766
Everyone in the comments says it is useful

7
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:13,166
You do not have time to watch closely

8
00:00:13,266 --> 00:00:14,966
So you save it first

9
00:00:15,166 --> 00:00:16,466
For a second you feel reassured

10
00:00:16,566 --> 00:00:18,366
As if you had not missed anything

11
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:19,366
But a few days later

12
00:00:19,366 --> 00:00:20,833
You open your saved folder again

13
00:00:20,933 --> 00:00:22,600
And find hundreds of videos inside

14
00:00:22,866 --> 00:00:23,866
Study methods

15
00:00:23,866 --> 00:00:24,900
Money making ideas

16
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:25,866
Emotional management

17
00:00:25,866 --> 00:00:26,800
AI tools

18
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:27,833
Communication skills

19
00:00:27,933 --> 00:00:29,566
Every one looks important

20
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:31,833
And every one is waiting for your future self to deal with it

21
00:00:31,866 --> 00:00:32,666
The problem is

22
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:33,966
Your future self will get tired too

23
00:00:33,966 --> 00:00:34,800
And busy too

24
00:00:34,866 --> 00:00:36,833
And keep running into new useful tips

25
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:38,866
So many people are not learning

26
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,166
They are just stockpiling the possibility of learning

27
00:00:41,333 --> 00:00:44,066
This is where the save button is most deceptive

28
00:00:44,066 --> 00:00:45,333
It takes only one second

29
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:48,066
To give you the feeling that you did not waste your time

30
00:00:48,166 --> 00:00:50,066
But knowledge does not become your judgment

31
00:00:50,166 --> 00:00:51,600
just because it was saved

32
00:00:51,666 --> 00:00:53,066
It has to be repeated by you

33
00:00:53,066 --> 00:00:53,666
used

34
00:00:53,666 --> 00:00:54,566
rewritten

35
00:00:54,566 --> 00:00:56,366
and taken to solve a specific problem

36
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:58,366
before it slowly becomes yours

37
00:00:58,566 --> 00:01:01,166
This video is not asking you to empty your saved folder

38
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:03,433
It only wants to help you see one boundary clearly

39
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:06,033
A saved item that answers a problem in front of you is a tool

40
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:08,466
One saved only for later is a bill you owe

41
00:01:08,533 --> 00:01:10,800
After watching you only need to ask one more question

42
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:13,933
What immediate problem am I going to solve with this

43
00:01:14,266 --> 00:01:15,666
Eleven thirty at night

44
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:18,166
You only meant to scroll on your phone for a while

45
00:01:18,466 --> 00:01:20,966
But you came across someone explaining study methods

46
00:01:21,266 --> 00:01:22,466
He says three sentences

47
00:01:22,666 --> 00:01:24,166
And suddenly you think he is right

48
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:25,433
You keep scrolling

49
00:01:25,533 --> 00:01:27,733
Then you see someone talking about time management

50
00:01:27,733 --> 00:01:28,600
That sounds right too

51
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:29,500
You scroll again

52
00:01:29,533 --> 00:01:31,066
Someone talks about asking AI questions

53
00:01:31,133 --> 00:01:32,466
Someone talks about content creation

54
00:01:32,466 --> 00:01:34,033
Someone talks about staying emotionally steady

55
00:01:34,133 --> 00:01:35,566
The more you scroll

56
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:37,800
The more you feel there is so much worth learning in the world

57
00:01:38,066 --> 00:01:39,633
So you keep saving

58
00:01:39,866 --> 00:01:40,866
Your fingers are busy

59
00:01:40,966 --> 00:01:42,066
And your mind is busy too

60
00:01:42,266 --> 00:01:44,000
You even feel a little satisfied

61
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:45,600
At least I did not scroll for nothing today

62
00:01:45,666 --> 00:01:47,266
I still learned something

63
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:48,766
But think about it carefully

64
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:49,933
The act of saving

65
00:01:49,933 --> 00:01:51,133
is really too easy

66
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:53,166
So easy it takes almost no effort

67
00:01:53,300 --> 00:01:54,266
No need to understand

68
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:55,266
No need to remember

69
00:01:55,266 --> 00:01:56,266
No need to argue with it

70
00:01:56,366 --> 00:01:57,400
No need to rewrite it

71
00:01:57,500 --> 00:01:59,200
No consequences to take responsibility for

72
00:01:59,366 --> 00:02:00,666
You only moved a piece of content

73
00:02:00,666 --> 00:02:02,933
From the feed into your saved folder

74
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:04,066
Like moving a piece of clothing

75
00:02:04,066 --> 00:02:06,166
From a store window into your shopping cart

76
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:08,200
But you have not worn it yet

77
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:10,166
And it has not become part of your life

78
00:02:10,266 --> 00:02:12,533
This is where saving is most deceptive

79
00:02:12,666 --> 00:02:14,666
It looks like the beginning of learning

80
00:02:14,766 --> 00:02:15,733
But much of the time

81
00:02:15,733 --> 00:02:17,333
It is also where learning ends

82
00:02:17,366 --> 00:02:18,866
Once you tap save

83
00:02:18,933 --> 00:02:20,366
Your brain lets out a sigh of relief

84
00:02:20,533 --> 00:02:22,733
As if the problem had already been handled

85
00:02:22,733 --> 00:02:24,633
As if the idea had already been preserved

86
00:02:24,733 --> 00:02:26,600
As if you had not missed the opportunity

87
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:27,633
But in reality

88
00:02:27,666 --> 00:02:29,466
You only pushed the time to deal with the problem

89
00:02:29,466 --> 00:02:30,533
onto your future self

90
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:31,500
Your future self

91
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:32,466
will have to open it

92
00:02:32,533 --> 00:02:33,400
understand it

93
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:34,233
organize it

94
00:02:34,266 --> 00:02:35,066
use it

95
00:02:35,066 --> 00:02:36,766
and remember why it mattered

96
00:02:36,766 --> 00:02:38,266
One light tap today

97
00:02:38,333 --> 00:02:41,000
gives your future self one more unfinished task

98
00:02:41,166 --> 00:02:42,533
Many people's saved folders

99
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:43,733
are not knowledge bases

100
00:02:43,866 --> 00:02:45,366
They are more like debt ledgers

101
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:46,966
Every item is saying

102
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,033
Look at it later

103
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:49,133
Learn it later

104
00:02:49,133 --> 00:02:50,066
Use it later

105
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:52,066
But what people most often overestimate

106
00:02:52,066 --> 00:02:53,766
is their future energy

107
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:55,566
If you save a resource

108
00:02:55,700 --> 00:02:57,666
for a project you are working on right now

109
00:02:57,700 --> 00:02:59,600
and you will use it right after saving it

110
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:01,366
there is nothing wrong with that

111
00:03:01,566 --> 00:03:03,000
What really holds people back

112
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:03,933
is saving without a clear problem in mind

113
00:03:04,166 --> 00:03:05,466
no time to use it

114
00:03:05,466 --> 00:03:06,833
and no specific purpose

115
00:03:06,933 --> 00:03:08,733
with only a promise to look at it later

116
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:10,800
You think your future self will be clearer

117
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:11,600
more disciplined

118
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:12,600
and have more time

119
00:03:12,733 --> 00:03:13,800
But your future self

120
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:15,200
will probably be working overtime too

121
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:16,433
scrolling on the phone too

122
00:03:16,533 --> 00:03:18,766
and getting pulled away by new content too

123
00:03:18,933 --> 00:03:20,800
So the saved folder keeps getting thicker

124
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:22,533
while your mind does not get any clearer

125
00:03:22,566 --> 00:03:24,000
Because what truly wears you out

126
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:25,600
is not that you failed to see good things

127
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:27,733
It is that you saw too many good things

128
00:03:27,933 --> 00:03:29,366
but did not turn a single one

129
00:03:29,366 --> 00:03:30,633
into something of your own

130
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:32,666
Psychology has a term for this

131
00:03:32,966 --> 00:03:34,266
It is called cognitive offloading

132
00:03:34,733 --> 00:03:36,000
In plain language

133
00:03:36,366 --> 00:03:38,766
When you know the information is stored in your phone

134
00:03:38,966 --> 00:03:40,833
your brain no longer has to keep holding it

135
00:03:41,166 --> 00:03:42,800
That is normally a good thing

136
00:03:43,133 --> 00:03:44,666
Navigation remembers the route for you

137
00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:46,400
A calendar remembers appointments for you

138
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:48,266
A notes app remembers your list for you

139
00:03:48,466 --> 00:03:49,333
The problem is

140
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:51,633
A saved folder can store an address for you

141
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:53,400
but it cannot complete the understanding for you

142
00:03:53,566 --> 00:03:55,433
You may only remember that you saved it

143
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:57,600
without being able to say what it was actually about

144
00:03:57,766 --> 00:03:59,166
Behind saving useful content

145
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:01,000
there is often another kind of anxiety

146
00:04:01,133 --> 00:04:02,400
You are afraid of falling behind

147
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:03,633
afraid of missing an opportunity

148
00:04:03,766 --> 00:04:05,033
afraid that everyone else knows

149
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:06,333
while you are the only one who does not

150
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:08,366
So when you see something useful

151
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:10,033
your first reaction is not to ask

152
00:04:10,166 --> 00:04:12,400
How does this relate to my problem right now

153
00:04:12,533 --> 00:04:13,666
You save it first

154
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:15,433
What if I need it someday

155
00:04:15,566 --> 00:04:16,533
This what if

156
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:18,600
is where much knowledge hoarding begins

157
00:04:18,733 --> 00:04:19,800
Someone buys a book

158
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:21,400
not because they plan to read it tonight

159
00:04:21,533 --> 00:04:23,666
but because they fear that edition will go out of print

160
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:24,833
Someone signs up for a course

161
00:04:24,933 --> 00:04:26,400
not because they need it this month

162
00:04:26,533 --> 00:04:28,366
but because they fear the discount will end

163
00:04:28,533 --> 00:04:29,733
Someone saves a video

164
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:31,933
not because it solves a problem right now

165
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:35,233
but because they fear missing a chance to become better

166
00:04:35,366 --> 00:04:36,766
It is a lot like hoarding things

167
00:04:36,966 --> 00:04:38,966
Your home already has plenty of cups

168
00:04:39,066 --> 00:04:40,900
But when you see one that is cheap and attractive

169
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:41,833
you still want to buy it

170
00:04:41,933 --> 00:04:43,566
Because you are not buying a cup

171
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:45,966
You are buying the reassurance that you will use it someday

172
00:04:46,166 --> 00:04:47,733
Saving knowledge works the same way

173
00:04:47,766 --> 00:04:49,600
You are not saving that piece of content

174
00:04:49,666 --> 00:04:51,800
You are saving an imagined version of yourself

175
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:53,733
The version who will wake up early later

176
00:04:53,733 --> 00:04:54,866
review things seriously

177
00:04:55,100 --> 00:04:57,200
turn every useful tip into notes

178
00:04:57,333 --> 00:04:59,433
put every idea to work

179
00:04:59,466 --> 00:05:01,533
and become a more capable person

180
00:05:01,566 --> 00:05:02,833
It sounds beautiful

181
00:05:02,933 --> 00:05:04,066
But here is the problem

182
00:05:04,166 --> 00:05:05,366
If today's you

183
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:07,600
has not truly processed even one piece of content

184
00:05:07,666 --> 00:05:08,533
why would your future self

185
00:05:08,533 --> 00:05:10,533
suddenly be able to process hundreds

186
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:12,433
More knowledge does not always mean more safety

187
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:13,333
A lot of the time

188
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:14,466
too much knowledge

189
00:05:14,533 --> 00:05:16,433
only makes people more afraid to act

190
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:17,733
You want to write an article

191
00:05:17,866 --> 00:05:19,466
so you save ten writing methods

192
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:22,133
After reading them you find everyone says something different

193
00:05:22,266 --> 00:05:23,766
Some say start with the hook

194
00:05:23,866 --> 00:05:25,400
Some say start with the structure

195
00:05:25,533 --> 00:05:27,000
Some say do not use structure

196
00:05:27,066 --> 00:05:28,533
Some say just be authentic

197
00:05:28,666 --> 00:05:29,800
The more you read

198
00:05:29,966 --> 00:05:31,366
the more confused you get

199
00:05:31,466 --> 00:05:32,666
And in the end you write not a single word

200
00:05:32,766 --> 00:05:34,166
You want to learn AI

201
00:05:34,266 --> 00:05:36,466
so you save twenty tools

202
00:05:36,566 --> 00:05:37,633
Every tool claims it can double your efficiency

203
00:05:37,733 --> 00:05:39,133
You try them one by one

204
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:41,166
and end up with a pile of registered accounts

205
00:05:41,300 --> 00:05:43,400
while very few real problems get solved

206
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:44,666
At this point knowledge no longer helps you

207
00:05:44,733 --> 00:05:46,666
It starts blocking you

208
00:05:46,666 --> 00:05:48,433
Because you do not have a real task

209
00:05:48,566 --> 00:05:49,733
to filter which knowledge is useful

210
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:51,666
Without a task

211
00:05:51,766 --> 00:05:52,833
every useful tip looks important

212
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:53,966
With a task

213
00:05:54,066 --> 00:05:55,600
you finally know

214
00:05:55,800 --> 00:05:57,666
which one can help you cross the bridge

215
00:05:57,800 --> 00:05:59,400
and which one only looks exciting

216
00:05:59,566 --> 00:06:01,633
So saving is not wrong

217
00:06:01,766 --> 00:06:04,266
The mistake is treating saving as learning

218
00:06:04,366 --> 00:06:05,533
At most it only puts something at the door

219
00:06:05,566 --> 00:06:06,966
Real learning

220
00:06:06,966 --> 00:06:07,766
means carrying it into the room

221
00:06:07,766 --> 00:06:08,566
taking it apart

222
00:06:08,600 --> 00:06:11,033
trying it out

223
00:06:11,666 --> 00:06:13,366
and seeing whether it can solve the problem in your hands

224
00:06:13,733 --> 00:06:16,266
You may have had this awkward experience

225
00:06:16,500 --> 00:06:17,800
A video feels incredibly powerful while you watch it

226
00:06:17,966 --> 00:06:19,400
Every sentence seems right

227
00:06:19,733 --> 00:06:20,800
You even send it to a friend

228
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:22,200
But when your friend asks

229
00:06:22,333 --> 00:06:23,433
What was it actually about

230
00:06:23,566 --> 00:06:25,133
You open your mouth

231
00:06:25,333 --> 00:06:26,133
and suddenly only a few words remain

232
00:06:26,133 --> 00:06:26,866
Anxiety

233
00:06:26,866 --> 00:06:27,600
growth

234
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:28,333
fundamentals

235
00:06:28,533 --> 00:06:30,233
methods

236
00:06:30,300 --> 00:06:31,633
You realize you seem to understand it

237
00:06:31,900 --> 00:06:33,600
and yet cannot explain it

238
00:06:33,966 --> 00:06:34,733
This is not a problem with your communication skills

239
00:06:34,733 --> 00:06:36,066
Much of the time

240
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:37,666
it is because that knowledge

241
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:39,600
only passed in front of your eyes

242
00:06:39,933 --> 00:06:41,266
It never truly stopped in your mind

243
00:06:41,266 --> 00:06:43,066
There is a long road between understanding something

244
00:06:43,166 --> 00:06:44,966
and being able to say it in your own words

245
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:46,433
Understanding is easy

246
00:06:46,566 --> 00:06:48,933
because the author has already paved the road for you

247
00:06:49,066 --> 00:06:50,166
They give you the opening

248
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:51,133
the examples

249
00:06:51,133 --> 00:06:52,133
the turns

250
00:06:52,133 --> 00:06:53,033
and the conclusion

251
00:06:53,133 --> 00:06:54,100
You simply follow along

252
00:06:54,100 --> 00:06:55,133
so of course it feels smooth

253
00:06:55,333 --> 00:06:57,033
But when you try to explain it yourself

254
00:06:57,200 --> 00:06:59,366
you have to pave that road again

255
00:06:59,533 --> 00:07:00,966
You have to know what to say first

256
00:07:00,966 --> 00:07:01,933
and what to say next

257
00:07:01,933 --> 00:07:02,866
where to give an example

258
00:07:02,933 --> 00:07:03,933
where to pause

259
00:07:03,933 --> 00:07:05,200
and where the real point is

260
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:06,100
That is when

261
00:07:06,133 --> 00:07:07,966
knowledge truly starts to feel heavy

262
00:07:08,266 --> 00:07:09,833
Many people dislike producing anything

263
00:07:09,933 --> 00:07:11,400
not because output is useless

264
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:13,466
but because output exposes them

265
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:14,300
The moment you write

266
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:15,833
you know what you do not understand

267
00:07:15,866 --> 00:07:16,666
The moment you explain

268
00:07:16,666 --> 00:07:18,800
you know where you were only nodding along

269
00:07:18,966 --> 00:07:19,766
The moment you use it

270
00:07:19,766 --> 00:07:22,033
you know how far the method is from reality

271
00:07:22,333 --> 00:07:23,866
Saving does not expose you

272
00:07:24,066 --> 00:07:25,600
Liking does not expose you

273
00:07:25,700 --> 00:07:26,833
Sharing does not either

274
00:07:26,933 --> 00:07:28,066
Only output does

275
00:07:28,266 --> 00:07:29,933
That is exactly why it is valuable

276
00:07:30,066 --> 00:07:31,466
You do not need to write a long article

277
00:07:31,566 --> 00:07:33,333
and you do not need to give a public speech

278
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:34,766
The simplest kind of output

279
00:07:34,866 --> 00:07:36,666
is to explain one saved item

280
00:07:36,666 --> 00:07:38,466
to yourself in three sentences

281
00:07:38,700 --> 00:07:39,500
First sentence

282
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:40,733
What is it saying

283
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:41,566
Second sentence

284
00:07:41,566 --> 00:07:42,766
What problem does it solve

285
00:07:43,100 --> 00:07:43,933
Third sentence

286
00:07:43,933 --> 00:07:45,566
Where can I use it today

287
00:07:45,666 --> 00:07:47,333
If you cannot write the third sentence

288
00:07:47,400 --> 00:07:48,966
this content is only pleasant to hear

289
00:07:49,066 --> 00:07:50,400
for now

290
00:07:50,466 --> 00:07:51,866
It has not become useful yet

291
00:07:52,100 --> 00:07:54,200
You save a method for managing emotions

292
00:07:54,266 --> 00:07:55,566
The third sentence could be

293
00:07:55,700 --> 00:07:57,400
Next time my idea gets rejected in a meeting

294
00:07:57,500 --> 00:07:59,433
I will delay my reaction by ten seconds first

295
00:07:59,666 --> 00:08:01,433
You save a writing method

296
00:08:01,533 --> 00:08:02,833
The third sentence could be

297
00:08:02,966 --> 00:08:04,433
For tonight's script

298
00:08:04,466 --> 00:08:05,833
I will revise the first three lines first

299
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:07,766
You save an AI technique

300
00:08:07,900 --> 00:08:09,200
The third sentence could be

301
00:08:09,300 --> 00:08:10,666
Before writing a proposal tomorrow

302
00:08:10,766 --> 00:08:13,066
I will ask AI to list the objections first

303
00:08:13,300 --> 00:08:14,466
Only at this point

304
00:08:14,566 --> 00:08:16,166
Knowledge moves from someone else's world

305
00:08:16,166 --> 00:08:17,566
into your life

306
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:18,633
Without output

307
00:08:18,733 --> 00:08:20,600
knowledge is like a roadside billboard

308
00:08:20,766 --> 00:08:21,700
You see it

309
00:08:21,700 --> 00:08:22,933
and think it makes sense

310
00:08:22,966 --> 00:08:24,133
But after you walk past it

311
00:08:24,166 --> 00:08:26,000
it will not change your direction for you

312
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:27,200
Tonight before bed

313
00:08:27,266 --> 00:08:28,666
you open your saved folder again

314
00:08:28,933 --> 00:08:31,966
Hundreds of unfinished items appear on the screen together

315
00:08:32,166 --> 00:08:34,266
Do not start catching up from the first one

316
00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:36,466
And do not rush to build a new system

317
00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:38,433
The next time you want to tap save

318
00:08:38,566 --> 00:08:39,733
just spend ten seconds first

319
00:08:39,733 --> 00:08:40,800
finishing one sentence

320
00:08:40,966 --> 00:08:42,533
What situation am I preparing to use it in

321
00:08:42,566 --> 00:08:43,933
and what problem will it solve

322
00:08:44,300 --> 00:08:45,766
If you cannot finish that sentence

323
00:08:45,866 --> 00:08:47,866
let it pass through the feed

324
00:08:47,966 --> 00:08:49,233
Not because it is bad

325
00:08:49,333 --> 00:08:51,466
but because it has nothing to do with you right now

326
00:08:51,666 --> 00:08:53,966
You can give yourself one tiny rule

327
00:08:54,133 --> 00:08:56,033
Process only one saved item a day

328
00:08:56,200 --> 00:08:57,366
Not ten

329
00:08:57,466 --> 00:08:58,766
Not all of them

330
00:08:58,900 --> 00:09:01,200
And not a whole knowledge management system

331
00:09:01,300 --> 00:09:02,133
Just one

332
00:09:02,266 --> 00:09:03,066
Open it

333
00:09:03,066 --> 00:09:03,866
finish it

334
00:09:03,900 --> 00:09:05,366
write it down in three sentences

335
00:09:05,466 --> 00:09:07,633
Then find one small situation where you can use it today

336
00:09:07,700 --> 00:09:08,600
If you cannot find one

337
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:09,433
delete it

338
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:10,433
Do not feel bad about it

339
00:09:10,533 --> 00:09:11,866
Deleting one irrelevant item

340
00:09:11,866 --> 00:09:12,766
is not wasteful

341
00:09:12,900 --> 00:09:15,533
Sometimes it is actually clearing space on your brain's desk

342
00:09:15,666 --> 00:09:17,800
Because the number of things you can handle at once

343
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:18,933
was always very small

344
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:20,166
The cleaner the desk

345
00:09:20,166 --> 00:09:22,400
the easier it is to see the one problem in front of you

346
00:09:22,733 --> 00:09:24,333
A lot of content is not bad

347
00:09:24,333 --> 00:09:25,833
It is just irrelevant to you right now

348
00:09:26,066 --> 00:09:27,166
Good things that are irrelevant

349
00:09:27,366 --> 00:09:29,166
become a burden when piled up

350
00:09:29,300 --> 00:09:30,133
A person's mind

351
00:09:30,133 --> 00:09:31,133
is not a warehouse

352
00:09:31,133 --> 00:09:32,166
It is more like a kitchen

353
00:09:32,333 --> 00:09:33,366
No matter how many ingredients you have

354
00:09:33,366 --> 00:09:34,133
if they never go into the pot

355
00:09:34,133 --> 00:09:35,200
they will never become a meal

356
00:09:35,366 --> 00:09:36,400
No matter how many recipes you have

357
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:37,100
if you never put your hands to work

358
00:09:37,100 --> 00:09:38,333
they will never become a skill

359
00:09:38,466 --> 00:09:39,633
Knowledge works the same way

360
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:41,833
You do not need to keep every good thing

361
00:09:41,933 --> 00:09:43,833
You only need to let a few things

362
00:09:44,066 --> 00:09:45,566
truly change your judgment

363
00:09:45,700 --> 00:09:47,166
Your saved folder can be thin

364
00:09:47,266 --> 00:09:48,666
as long as you have used every item in it

365
00:09:48,766 --> 00:09:50,333
That looks much more like learning

366
00:09:50,333 --> 00:09:51,400
than thousands of items lying there

367
00:09:51,533 --> 00:09:52,800
Truly understanding something

368
00:09:53,066 --> 00:09:54,966
is not about the day you happened to see it

369
00:09:55,100 --> 00:09:56,633
It is about a specific moment

370
00:09:56,766 --> 00:09:57,933
when you face a problem

371
00:09:58,133 --> 00:09:59,266
and suddenly remember it

372
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:01,633
then use it to make a better judgment

373
00:10:01,733 --> 00:10:03,433
For example you are about to impulsively sign up for a course

374
00:10:03,533 --> 00:10:05,833
and remember that you have saved many courses before

375
00:10:05,933 --> 00:10:07,433
but finished very few of them

376
00:10:07,566 --> 00:10:08,666
So you ask first

377
00:10:08,766 --> 00:10:11,166
What specific problem am I trying to solve this time

378
00:10:11,466 --> 00:10:13,633
For example you want to save a communication tip

379
00:10:13,733 --> 00:10:15,366
You do not just tap save anymore

380
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:17,766
Instead you immediately take what you plan to say tonight

381
00:10:17,800 --> 00:10:19,366
and revise it using that method

382
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:21,433
For example you see a study method

383
00:10:21,533 --> 00:10:23,333
You do not tell yourself you will try it someday

384
00:10:23,333 --> 00:10:25,266
Instead you pick up the book you are reading

385
00:10:25,366 --> 00:10:26,466
and write down three sentences

386
00:10:26,566 --> 00:10:28,566
That is what it means to bring knowledge back into life

387
00:10:28,666 --> 00:10:29,966
You will slowly notice

388
00:10:30,100 --> 00:10:31,333
A thicker saved folder

389
00:10:31,366 --> 00:10:33,133
does not necessarily make you feel more confident

390
00:10:33,266 --> 00:10:34,400
Real confidence

391
00:10:34,500 --> 00:10:35,600
comes from knowing

392
00:10:35,666 --> 00:10:37,566
which things you have actually used

393
00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:39,233
and which things merely passed by

394
00:10:39,400 --> 00:10:40,800
The next time you come across a useful tip

395
00:10:40,900 --> 00:10:42,066
let your finger pause

396
00:10:42,166 --> 00:10:44,166
Do not rush to throw it into your saved folder

397
00:10:44,333 --> 00:10:45,933
Complete that sentence first

398
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:47,433
What situation will I use it in

399
00:10:47,466 --> 00:10:48,833
What problem will it solve

400
00:10:49,133 --> 00:10:50,466
If it cannot answer

401
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:51,333
let it go

402
00:10:51,366 --> 00:10:53,233
A person truly begins learning

403
00:10:53,300 --> 00:10:55,566
often not by saving more

404
00:10:55,566 --> 00:10:58,566
but by letting go of most seemingly useful things
